architecture.projection:
State: change `const cam = { x:0, y:0, z:1 }` → `{ x:0, y:0, z:1, r:0 }` (render.js:4). Let N=MAP. Add two pure helpers directly above worldX (render.js:79), with an early `r===0` identity return so the r=0 path is provably arithmetic-free:

  const rot = (x,y,r) => r===0?{u:x,v:y}: r===1?{u:y,v:N-1-x}: r===2?{u:N-1-x,v:N-1-y}: {u:N-1-y,v:x};
  const unrot = (u,v,r) => r===0?{x:u,y:v}: r===1?{x:N-1-v,y:u}: r===2?{x:N-1-u,y:N-1-v}: {x:v,y:N-1-u};

These are exact inverses (assert rot∘unrot = identity over all 4 r including the N-1 boundary tiles). Forward — the ONE place rotation lives:
  worldX(x,y): const {u,v}=rot(x,y,cam.r); return (u-v)*HW;
  worldY(x,y): const {u,v}=rot(x,y,cam.r); return (u+v)*HH+HH;
Inverse (screenToTile render.js:226-231): keep A=wx/HW, B=(wy-HH)/HH; recover FLOAT view coords u=(A+B)/2, v=(B-A)/2; Math.round u,v IN VIEW SPACE; then `const {x,y}=unrot(Math.round(u),Math.round(v),cam.r); return {x,y}`. Rounding before unrot is mandatory — rounding logical x,y after unrot mis-picks near diagonal seams.

cam.r is the ONLY orientation state; rotate = `cam.r=(cam.r+dir+4)&3`. rot/unrot live in render.js's global scope, already visible to sprites.js/ui.js/postcard.js/ambience.js (no modules). Functions that change: worldX, worldY, screenToTile, the painter + terrain loops, the 2x2 anchor block, cache keys tKey/nKey (append cam.r), and the TWO hidden inline-projection copies that must be re-routed (car headlight render.js:716-719, minimap viewport inverse render.js:1131-1140). Everything else inheriting worldX/worldY/screenToTile is untouched. Per-tile cost: one branch + ≤2 subtractions, cheaper than the multiply it feeds — negligible vs the drawImage.

architecture.painterOrder:
The painter loop (render.js:363-368, currently `for s=x+y … for x … y=s-x`) must group by VIEW depth u+v, because projected screen depth is monotonic in u+v and inverts for 3 of 4 orientations. Rewrite to walk the view diagonal and convert back to logical coords to fetch city data:

  for (let s=0; s<=(N-1)*2; s++){
    for (let u=Math.max(0,s-N+1); u<=Math.min(N-1,s); u++){
      const v=s-u; const {x,y}=unrot(u,v,cam.r); const i=y*MAP+x;
      ... existing body verbatim; wx=worldX(x,y), wy=worldY(x,y) re-rotate internally, consistent ...
    }
  }

Back-to-front is preserved under every r because s now equals projected depth. flushNightDiag (render.js: called per outer-s) needs ZERO change — it still fires exactly once per projected-depth band, the punch-before-add invariant it and drawNightLayer depend on. The two buildTerrainLayer loops (render.js:259-300) are order-INSENSITIVE (flat diamonds never occlude), so they need only the rotation-aware worldX/worldY — keep them iterating logical s=x+y; every logical tile is visited once regardless of order, no unrot needed there (mask rotation for shore/beach/terrEdge handled in masks). Forest checkerboard flip `(x+y)&1` (render.js:388) stays deterministic but reshuffles per r; optionally key it on a rotation-invariant hash for byte-stable variety across r — low priority.

2x2 anchor block (render.js:447-467): the one place corner selection changes. anc stays the logical min-corner; only its SCREEN role rotates. Of the four footprint corners {(ax,ay),(ax+sz-1,ay),(ax,ay+sz-1),(ax+sz-1,ay+sz-1)}, compute each corner's view depth via rot(...).u+.v. TRIGGER the draw when the loop's current (x,y) equals the argmax-depth corner (front-most, always in-footprint so always in-map — no edge-clip issue). POSITION from the argmin-depth corner (screen-back): (bx,by)=that corner; awx=worldX(bx,by), awy=worldY(bx,by). Each per-facing sprite bakes its ox/oy relative to that orientation's back corner, so sprite and anchor agree. The afire scan and nightPunch/nightAdd reuse the recomputed awx/awy.

architecture.masks:
RESOLUTION: lookup-time rotation, NOT neighbor re-probing (all three lenses agree; this is the cheap win — one helper, ~7 sites, zero rebake). roadMask/wireMask/shoreMask/beachMask/terrEdgeMask (sprites.js:1654,1731,1668,1681,1696) keep computing connectivity in WORLD/tile space unchanged — the road/water/wire graph is the same set of tiles at any camera angle; do NOT rotate the N/E/S/W probes. What rotates is which on-screen diamond edge shows a given world-neighbor, and that lives only at the 16-entry table index. Add:

  const rot4 = (m,r) => r===0 ? m : ((m << r) | (m >> (4-r))) & 15;

Wrap every table index with cam.r at the ~7 sites: spriteFor road/roadWinter (sprites.js:1629,1633), wire (1634); render.js overhead WIREROAD wire (SPR.wire[wireMask], the loop-body line ~407); terrain-layer shore (275), beach (282), terrEdge (298). E.g. `SPR.road[rot4(roadMask(city,i), cam.r)]`. The tables are fully rotationally symmetric families under one shared convention (bit0=N→NE screen edge, clockwise), so the rotated index always lands on an already-baked sprite. Straight detection (m===5||m===10) survives bit-rotation (5↔10, both handled). The ONE empirical thing to pin: rol vs ror (`<<` vs `>>`) — verify against a single 90° turn (a road tee must point its stem at the rotated screen edge, a straight N-S road must read the correct screen diagonal); if wrong, swap to `((m>>r)|(m<<(4-r)))&15`. CRITICAL: this turn direction must be DERIVED from / agree with rot()/unrot() so masks and buildings rotate the same visual way — do it once empirically then it's fixed. The same rot4 serves all five families. The 2x2 anchor facing selection couples here: spriteFor(city,a,cam.r) picks the facing whose two visible faces match the two screen faces the rotated footprint presents — designed together with the anchor corner math (painterOrder).
